    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, our offensive core is heavily anchored by the favorable matchup against Ryan Feltner, whose projected efficiency provides a significant tailwind for **Luis Arraez** (66.77), **Jung Hoo Lee** (65.64), and **Matt Chapman** (70.20). **Bryson Stott** commands the highest score of the day (72.45) as he faces Sasaki; the combination of a high-ceiling opposing xERA (5.11) and a positive platoon split makes him our must-start anchor. **Daulton Varsho** (72.31) mirrors this excellence, benefiting from an elite park environment and a favorable matchup against Young. These plays capitalize on our \"SB priority\" protocol, as the favorable delivery profiles against **Drew Gilbert**, **Jung Hoo Lee**, and **Matt Chapman** create a high-probability environment for stolen base attempts, a critical edge in our 5x5 chase.\n\nThe bench decision process was tightest between **Masyn Winn** (41.17) and **Geraldo Perdomo** (44.56). While **Masyn Winn** offers an interesting speed profile, his matchup against Hunter Brown carries too much SP-skill risk (-15%), ultimately pushing him below our efficiency threshold. **Geraldo Perdomo** narrowly secured the start despite his \"Lineup Pending\" status because his projected role in the #3 spot provides superior counting-stat volume over the alternatives. We are monitoring the status of **Geraldo Perdomo** closely; should he be dropped from the lineup, we will pivot to more stable production to ensure we aren't burning a roster slot. \n\nWe must remain vigilant regarding **Will Smith** (43.22) and **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (56.83), as both face tough arms in Luzardo and Gray, respectively. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** remains a start due to his pedigree, but his BvP history against Gray is a slight concern that lowered his base projection. Conversely, **Taylor Ward** (58.22) and **Jeremiah Jackson** (50.21) offer high-variance outcomes against Yesavage, but the park factors keep them in the 50-60 range. 